  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...\nAs the closed upper low continues to steer eastward across our area \nand the Great Basin, continuous valley rain and mountain snow \nshowers will be expected through most of Thursday as the \nprecipitation tapers off east over southwest ID late Thursday \nthrough early Friday. Lower valley areas are tentatively looking at \naround 0.20 inches of total rainfall by early Friday. Latest long-\nrange guidance has snow levels hanging in the 3500-4500 ft range \nthrough Saturday. Valleys can see a rain/snow mix Friday morning, \nbefore precipitation completely tapers off by late Friday. Mountain \npeaks can still see possible total snow accumulations of 5 to 10 \ninches by early Saturday. Temperatures are expected to hover near or \nslightly below normal Thursday through the weekend. Come early \nSaturday, the system will steer out of our area as a ridge builds in \nbehind it. This brief dry period will be limited, as the latest long-\nterm guidance now has another Pacific upper trough moving into the \nnorthwest and into the eastern OR side by late Saturday. Come \nSunday, there is uncertainty over whether the trough will bring a \nhighly moist shortwave and return widespread precipitation or steer \nnorth of our area and bring limited precipitation. For now, slight \nto moderate chances of valley rain or rain/snow mix and mountain \nsnow showers are forecasted for late Saturday through early Monday, \nwith higher terrain areas seeing the bulk of total precip.",
